<resources>
    <!--
        Modal sizing. Every dialog is built through ModalScale.wrap, which applies this overlay,
        so capping the width here caps all ~137 of them without touching a call site.

        No cap on a handset-sized panel: a Palma's screen IS the right width for a dialog. The
        override lives in values-sw600dp, where a stock AlertDialog otherwise stretches across a
        13" page and a four-line menu becomes a wall.
    -->
    <!--
        THE SETTINGS PAGE'S TYPE SYSTEM.

        Six styles, and between them they are the whole visual argument of that screen. The page
        is 1404x1872 of e-ink, which removes almost every lever a settings screen normally pulls:
        no animation, no elevation, no gradient, no translucency (alpha dithers into mud), and no
        colour worth the name — the panel is monochrome, so LedgerTheme's accent resolves to a
        grey there and can never carry meaning. Greys are worse than useless: above about #777
        they vanish into the paper, below about #333 they read as plain black. There is black,
        there is white, and there is the space between them.

        So hierarchy is built out of the four things that DO survive: rule weight, letter case,
        type size, and whitespace. That is also, not coincidentally, how the app's own drawn pages
        already work — CalendarDayPageIntake sets its quarter titles in mono caps above a solid
        2dp border, and LedgerContextMenu copies that for its popup header. These styles bring the
        settings screen into that same hand instead of leaving it in stock Material.
    -->

    <!--
        Section heading: mono, caps, full ink, sitting just under its rule.

        It used to be 13sp bold at #7A7A7A — precisely the grey that disappears on this panel, so
        the ten headings that organise the page were the faintest thing on it. Monospace is not
        decoration: it is the face the app's own page templates title their panels in, and the
        even advance of a mono cap is what makes a short heading read as a label rather than as a
        sentence. Contrast against the body text now comes from case and face, both of which are
        as legible at 3% battery in bad light as they are anywhere.

        Line spacing is generous because CalendarSettingsFragment.styleSectionHeadings breaks each
        of these onto two lines — NAME above, the "· gloss" below at 0.8 — and two mono lines set
        solid would tangle.
    -->
    <style name="Settings.SectionHeading" parent="">
        <item name="android:textSize">15sp</item>
        <!--
            NOT bold, and that is load-bearing: CalendarSettingsFragment.sectionHeading bolds the
            NAME with a StyleSpan and leaves the gloss plain, and StyleSpan only ever ORs weight
            onto the base. Set bold here and the span becomes a no-op, both lines come out heavy,
            and the two-line split stops meaning anything.
        -->
        <item name="android:fontFamily">monospace</item>
        <item name="android:textColor">@color/fgColor</item>
        <item name="android:letterSpacing">0.05</item>
        <item name="android:lineSpacingExtra">3dp</item>
        <item name="android:layout_marginTop">12dp</item>
        <item name="android:layout_marginBottom">4dp</item>
        <item name="android:layout_width">match_parent</item>
        <item name="android:layout_height">wrap_content</item>
    </style>

    <!--
        The section rule: 3dp of solid ink across the column, immediately above a heading.

        One weight, one meaning — a rule is a section boundary and nothing else. The old page had
        twelve rules at 2dp #e0e0e0, three of which merely separated one dropdown from the next
        dropdown inside a single section, and #e0e0e0 on e-ink is white. So the page had twelve
        invisible lines doing two different jobs. The three interior ones are gone (whitespace
        separates a label/dropdown pair perfectly well) and the nine that mark real boundaries are
        now heavy enough to see and count while scrolling.
    -->
    <style name="Settings.Rule" parent="">
        <item name="android:background">@color/fgColor</item>
        <item name="android:layout_width">match_parent</item>
        <item name="android:layout_height">3dp</item>
        <item name="android:layout_marginTop">22dp</item>
    </style>

    <!--
        A caption above a dropdown ("Select the start view", "Sync interval:"). Deliberately
        smaller than the switch labels it shares the page with: a caption names the control under
        it, a switch label IS the control, and on a page with no colour the only way to say which
        is which is size and proximity. Tight bottom margin so it belongs to the box below it
        rather than floating between two.
    -->
    <style name="Settings.FieldLabel" parent="">
        <item name="android:textSize">15sp</item>
        <item name="android:textColor">@color/fgColor</item>
        <item name="android:layout_marginTop">18dp</item>
        <item name="android:layout_marginBottom">6dp</item>
        <item name="android:layout_width">match_parent</item>
        <item name="android:layout_height">wrap_content</item>
    </style>

    <!--
        An explanatory paragraph — not a setting. Marked the way a printed page marks an aside:
        indented, with a rule down the left margin (drawable/settings_hint_bar). Grey would have
        been the reflex and is unavailable; italic would have been the second reflex and costs
        real legibility across four lines on e-ink. Position and a bar cost nothing and cannot be
        misread, and the text stays at full ink for the people who most need to read it.
    -->
    <style name="Settings.Hint" parent="">
        <item name="android:textSize">14sp</item>
        <item name="android:textColor">@color/fgColor</item>
        <item name="android:background">@drawable/settings_hint_bar</item>
        <item name="android:paddingStart">14dp</item>
        <item name="android:paddingTop">2dp</item>
        <item name="android:paddingBottom">2dp</item>
        <item name="android:lineSpacingExtra">3dp</item>
        <item name="android:layout_marginTop">10dp</item>
        <item name="android:layout_width">match_parent</item>
        <item name="android:layout_height">wrap_content</item>
    </style>

    <!--
        Switch and tick-box rows. Full width and 52dp tall because this is driven with a stylus or
        a thumb on a device with no hover and a ~300ms redraw — a missed tap costs a full page
        flash. 17sp matches the menu rows (ScreenFragment.ROW_SP), so a label here is the same
        size as a label in the ▦ directory rather than mysteriously smaller.
    -->
    <style name="Settings.Toggle" parent="">
        <item name="android:textSize">17sp</item>
        <item name="android:textColor">@color/fgColor</item>
        <item name="android:minHeight">52dp</item>
        <item name="android:gravity">center_vertical</item>
        <item name="android:layout_width">match_parent</item>
        <item name="android:layout_height">wrap_content</item>
    </style>

    <!--
        Every button on the page, at one width and one height.

        They were wrap_content, so nine buttons made nine different-length boxes down the left
        edge and the column had no edge at all. Full width gives the page a spine, gives the
        stylus a target it cannot miss, and lets the label sit at a readable size without the
        button growing to swallow the row. stateListAnimator is nulled because the platform's
        default is an elevation animation, and this app draws nothing that lifts off the paper.
    -->
    <style name="Settings.Button" parent="">
        <item name="android:background">@drawable/settings_button_bg</item>
        <item name="android:textColor">@color/settings_button_text</item>
        <item name="android:textSize">17sp</item>
        <item name="android:textAllCaps">false</item>
        <item name="android:stateListAnimator">@null</item>
        <item name="android:elevation">0dp</item>
        <item name="android:minHeight">54dp</item>
        <item name="android:paddingHorizontal">18dp</item>
        <item name="android:paddingVertical">12dp</item>
        <item name="android:layout_width">match_parent</item>
        <item name="android:layout_height">wrap_content</item>
        <item name="android:layout_marginTop">10dp</item>
    </style>

    <!--
        The filled variant, used exactly twice: Legibility at the top of the page and Save at the
        bottom of it. Ink coverage is the only emphasis this panel can render, so spending it
        twice is the whole budget — a third solid button and none of them would mean anything.
    -->
    <style name="Settings.Button.Primary" parent="Settings.Button">
        <item name="android:background">@drawable/settings_button_primary_bg</item>
        <item name="android:textColor">@color/settings_button_primary_text</item>
        <item name="android:textStyle">bold</item>
    </style>

    <!--
        The frame around a group that appears only when its switch is on. Padding is asymmetric on
        purpose: the fields inside already carry their own top margin, so the box needs less at the
        top than at the bottom to sit optically even.
    -->
    <style name="Settings.Group" parent="">
        <item name="android:background">@drawable/settings_group_frame</item>
        <item name="android:padding">14dp</item>
        <item name="android:layout_marginTop">10dp</item>
        <item name="android:layout_width">match_parent</item>
        <item name="android:layout_height">wrap_content</item>
    </style>

    <!--
        The closed fold that stands in for that box while the switch is off — see
        drawable/settings_fold_dashed for why it is dashed. Software layer because a dashed stroke
        is silently dropped when the view is drawn into a hardware layer; LedgerContextMenu's
        DashRule sets the same flag for the same reason.
    -->
    <style name="Settings.Fold" parent="">
        <item name="android:background">@drawable/settings_fold_dashed</item>
        <item name="android:layerType">software</item>
        <item name="android:layout_width">match_parent</item>
        <item name="android:layout_height">14dp</item>
        <item name="android:layout_marginTop">10dp</item>
    </style>

    <!-- Dialogs stay LIGHT cards even in Dark scheme (the full dark-dialog pass is deferred —
         e-ink regression risk). But with App.Theme on DayNight, dialog views built from the
         ACTIVITY context inherit near-white text colours in Dark mode and vanished on the light
         card. Pinning the dialog theme's text colours dark keeps every dialog self-consistent. -->
    <style name="LedgerAlertDialog" parent="Theme.AppCompat.Light.Dialog.Alert">
        <item name="android:textColorPrimary">#DE000000</item>
        <item name="android:textColorSecondary">#8A000000</item>
    </style>
    <style name="LedgerAlertDialog.Small"/>
    <style name="LedgerAlertDialog.Medium"/>
    <style name="LedgerAlertDialog.Large"/>

    <!-- One overlay per text-size tier: ModalScale picks the matching one, so the BOX narrows or
         widens with the setting instead of sitting at one fixed width nobody chose.
         The base overlay also pins text colours DARK: custom dialog views inflated from the
         wrapped context otherwise inherit the Dark scheme's near-white textColorPrimary and
         disappear against the light dialog card. -->
    <style name="LedgerModalOverlay" parent="">
        <item name="android:textColorPrimary">#DE000000</item>
        <item name="android:textColorSecondary">#8A000000</item>
    </style>
    <style name="LedgerModalOverlay.Small">
        <item name="alertDialogTheme">@style/LedgerAlertDialog.Small</item>
        <item name="android:alertDialogTheme">@style/LedgerAlertDialog.Small</item>
    </style>
    <style name="LedgerModalOverlay.Medium">
        <item name="alertDialogTheme">@style/LedgerAlertDialog.Medium</item>
        <item name="android:alertDialogTheme">@style/LedgerAlertDialog.Medium</item>
    </style>
    <style name="LedgerModalOverlay.Large">
        <item name="alertDialogTheme">@style/LedgerAlertDialog.Large</item>
        <item name="android:alertDialogTheme">@style/LedgerAlertDialog.Large</item>
    </style>

    <!-- DayNight so the values-night colour resources activate when the scheme (LedgerTheme) asks
         for dark. AppCompatDelegate's night mode, set at startup, drives which side is shown. -->
    <style name="App.Theme" parent="Theme.AppCompat.DayNight.NoActionBar">
        <item name="colorPrimary">@color/colorPrimary</item>
        <item name="colorPrimaryDark">@color/colorPrimaryDark</item>
        <item name="colorAccent">@color/colorAccent</item>
        <item name="android:textColorPrimary">@color/fgColor</item>
        <item name="android:textColorSecondary">@color/fgColor</item>
        <item name="android:fontFamily">@font/atkinson_hyperlegible</item>
    </style>

    <style name="App.FloatingActionButton" parent="@style/Widget.MaterialComponents.FloatingActionButton">
        <item name="tint">@color/fgColor</item>
        <item name="backgroundTint">@color/widgetColor</item>

        <item name="android:layout_gravity">end|bottom</item>
        <item name="android:layout_marginBottom">@dimen/fab_fg_margin</item>
        <item name="android:layout_marginTop">@dimen/fab_fg_margin</item>
        <item name="android:layout_marginLeft">@dimen/fab_fg_margin</item>
        <item name="android:layout_marginRight">@dimen/fab_fg_margin</item>
        <item name="android:adjustViewBounds">true</item>
    </style>

    <style name="App.Text.Headline6" parent="TextAppearance.MaterialComponents.Headline6">
    </style>

    <!-- Left drawer: the ledger directory slides in/out from the left edge. -->
    <style name="DrawerAnimation">
        <item name="android:windowEnterAnimation">@anim/drawer_enter</item>
        <item name="android:windowExitAnimation">@anim/drawer_exit</item>
    </style>
</resources>
